torch bakes C10_WARP_SIZE=32 into every kernel compiled for gfx803 (Polaris,
GCN3), because the macro returns 64 only for __GFX9__ and falls through to 32
for every older arch. Polaris hardware is wave64-only (no wave32), so any
kernel that uses C10_WARP_SIZE -- layer_norm, group_norm, softmax, the
block_reduce/Reduce warp reductions, attention, indexing, TopK, sorting --
reduces only 32 of the 64 lanes in each wave, dropping half the data. The
result is silent garbage or NaN (reproduced on an RX 570: a warp reduction
written against C10_WARP_SIZE=32 returns 32.0 for a 64-element sum of
ones; against 64 it returns 64.0). It is a compile-time constant baked into
libtorch's kernel binaries, so there is no runtime-side (HIP/HSA) substitute
-- the header must be fixed and torch rebuilt. Same wave64 class the repo's
triton patches already fix on the triton side (ISAFamily::GCN3 -> warpSize
64, patches/triton/). No other arch is affected: gfx9 stays 64, gfx10+/
NVIDIA stay 32.

--- a/torch/headeronly/macros/Macros.h
+++ b/torch/headeronly/macros/Macros.h
@@ -386,11 +386,11 @@ static __device__ inline int C10_WARP_SIZE_INTERNAL() {
 #else // __SPIRV__
 static __device__ inline constexpr int C10_WARP_SIZE_INTERNAL() {
-#if defined(__GFX9__)
+#if defined(__GFX8__) || defined(__GFX9__)
   return 64;
-#else // __GFX9__
+#else // __GFX8__ || __GFX9__
   return 32;
-#endif // __GFX9__
+#endif // __GFX8__ || __GFX9__
 }
 #endif // __SPIRV__
 #if defined(__SPIRV__)
 #define C10_WARP_SIZE_LOWER_BOUND 32
 #define C10_WARP_SIZE_UPPER_BOUND 64
-#elif defined(__GFX9__)
+#elif defined(__GFX8__) || defined(__GFX9__)
 #define C10_WARP_SIZE_LOWER_BOUND 64
 #define C10_WARP_SIZE_UPPER_BOUND 64
 #else
